WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.240
"Fasting-Mimicking Diet Before
and After Chemotherapy"

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.360
Short-term food withdrawal during
chemotherapy may begin to solve

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.480
the long-standing problem
with most cancer treatments:

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.520
how to kill the tumor
without killing the patient?

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.040
Short-term fasting --
for example,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.080
for 48 hours before chemo
and 24 hours afterwards --

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.080
may reduce side effects, reduce
chemotherapy-induced toxicity.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.560
However, the potential tumor-
suppressing effects of fasting --

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.280
for example, its influence on tumor
growth, metastasis and prognosis --

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.800
have not been evaluated until now.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.320
No, actually, sadly, they mean
"have not been evaluated until now,"

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.240
meaning they have not 
been evaluated yet, period.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.040
So in answering the question to fast
or not to fast before chemotherapy,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.920
it should be emphasized
that the evidence provided

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.520
by human studies
is still very limited.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.000
Some argue that reducing
the side effects alone

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.680
could improve efficacy,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.800
since patients could
withstand higher doses.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.440
For example, the heart and
kidney damage associated

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.560
with the widely prescribed
anti-cancer drugs limit

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.960
their full therapeutic potential.
It's not clear, though, that

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.640
maximizing the tolerated
chemo dose would achieve

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.120
longer survival or
better quality of life.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.840
For now, I think we should
just be satisfied with

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.320
the fewer side effects for
fewer side effects' sake.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.720
Some cancer docs urge caution,
concerned about malnutrition.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.280
That concern is echoed
in official guidelines

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.120
on nutrition in cancer patients.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.600
Because of the risks
of malnutrition and,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.480
yeah, maybe a day
or two might not hurt,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.240
but because patients might
be tempted to prolong fasting

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.080
episodes without firm evidence
of a benefit, they feel

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.960
fasting during chemotherapy
cannot be recommended.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.640
Fasting proponents agree that
patients with severe weight loss,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.080
muscle wasting or malnutrition --
probably not good candidates

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.080
for even short-term fasting.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.640
But out of the hundreds of
patients that have been tracked,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.240
there's no evidence of severe adverse
events in terms of malnutrition

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.480
or weight loss with short-term
fasting or fasting-mimicking diets.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.800
And those patients who did
experience a weight loss

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.440
during fasting typically
recovered their weight

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.000
before the subsequent cycle
of chemo without detectable harm.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.440
But look, even if there is a risk,
we're talking about cancer here.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.640
Accepting trade-offs is kind
of the name of the game.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.440
People are willing to put up
with the risk of severe,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.880
even life-threatening side
effects all the time.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.920
And even if it just helped
alleviate some of the side effects,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.160
it might be worth a try.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.680
Of course, meanwhile Big Pharma
is busy trying to come up with drugs

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.480
aimed at reproducing the
beneficial effects of fasting.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.000
Although fasting mimicking drugs
may be preferred by patients

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.520
who would rather pop a pill
than go without eating,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.360
it will be years before
they'll ever be on the market,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.320
and it's hard to imagine drugs
will be developed that are

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.640
both as effective and
as safe as fasting.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.200
So is there anything
we can tweak in our diet

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.960
to get similar benefits
without having to fast?

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.080
The way fasting works
is by reducing levels

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.200
of a cancer-promoting
growth hormone known as

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.040
insulin-like growth factor 1.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.080
It's the reduced levels
of IGF-1 that mediate

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.760
the differential protection
of normal and cancer cells

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.200
in response to fasting

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.840
and improves chemo's
ability to kill cancer

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.520
but spare normal cells.
How do we know this?

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.480
Because restoration of IGF-1
was sufficient to reverse

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.520
the protective effect of fasting.
Check it out.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.520
Here's the growth of two cancer cell
lines, breast cancer and melanoma,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.880
in a petri dish after being
exposed to chemotherapy.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.000
Survival rates dropped down
into the 60s or even 20s.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.080
But the same dose in starved
cells works even better.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.000
Same dose, same starvation,
but drip back a little IGF-1

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.520
and the starvation benefit vanishes.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.680
So reducing IGF-1 signaling
may provide the dual benefits

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.440
by protecting normal cells while
reducing tumor progression

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.520
and may even help prevent
the cancer in the first place.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.680
As I explained before in my
"How Not To Die from Cancer" video,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.040
and an important video
about animal protein --

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.680
which kind of gives away
the punch line --

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.120
but fasting isn't the only way
to drop IGF-1 levels.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.720
Yes, a few days of fasting
can cut levels in half,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.160
but that's largely because
you're cutting your protein intake.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.480
Protein is a key determinant of
circulating IGF-1 levels in humans,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.560
suggesting that reduced
protein intake may become

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.240
an important component
of anticancer and anti-aging

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:04.000
dietary interventions, particularly
a reduction in animal protein.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.200
If you compare those eating strictly
plant-based diets, getting about the

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.760
recommended daily intake of protein,
0.8 grams per kg of body weight,

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.720
and compare them to people just
as slender but eating more typical

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.680
American protein intake, going
on a calorie-restricted diet

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.320
may lower IGF-1 a little, but eating
a plant-based diet can lower it more.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.800
So not only may a diet centered
around whole plant foods

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.728
down-regulate IGF-1 activity,
potentially slowing the aging process,

00:05:31.728 --> 00:05:38.467
but may be a way of turning
anti-aging genes against cancer.

